---
import PathComparisonRows from "../../../components/studies/PathComparisonRows.svelte";
import PathTraceDemo from "../../../components/studies/PathTraceDemo.svelte";
import PathViewDemo from "../../../components/studies/PathViewDemo.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "path-instrument-2026-11-01",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section aria-labelledby="closure">
    <h2 id="closure">Closed / Incomplete and failed</h2>
    <p>
      Closed on 1 November 2026 at the designer’s request. The study is
      incomplete and is recorded as failed: it did not reach readiness or
      integration, and the later layout and container work did not relate to the
      questions the study set out to investigate. The sections below are kept as
      they were, as evidence and not as approved behaviour.
    </p>
    <h3>Results the designer judged useful</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>02.1:</strong>
        drawing two studies on one fixed set of scales makes them comparable;
        fitting axes per study does not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>02.2:</strong>
        the path view has two formats, fit all paths and a default scale that
        can be panned. Both worked as they were; the default scale value was not
        decided.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>02.3:</strong>
        a time slider with a marker and a tick across the path and a cursor
        across every trace; tenth-of-a-second and hundredth-of-a-second steps
        were both useful.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Not settled</h3>
    <p>
      Trace scaling, how configurations and path sources are chosen, the
      contents of the side pane, and whether the composed instrument reads well
      on an iPad and in the Lab. Container behaviour, the single side pane and
      snapping into place were given in the brief, not investigated. The shell
      drafted after 02.3 is not shown on this page.
    </p>
    <p>
      The run is documented in the
      <code>docs/loop-evidence/2026-11-01-path-instrument-closure.md</code>
      record. No integration is approved by closure.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="situation">
    <h2 id="situation">01 / One ruler for every motion study</h2>
    <p>
      Studies of ship velocity, mass or engine thrust all ask the same kind of
      question: how does this configuration move compared with that one? A
      comparison only means something if both were measured the same way. When
      each study invents its own instrumentation, with its own scales, input
      sequence, graphs and framing, results from different studies cannot be set
      side by side, and often the two halves of a single study cannot either.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the instrument is a fixed way of measuring, not a convenience. It
      calculates paths and draws them as static pictures, with no flying,
      playback or animation. Studies supply the configurations; they do not
      redefine the measurement. Paths and their sources can vary, while the
      views, scales, units and input conventions stay the same everywhere the
      instrument appears.
    </p>
    <p>
      Picture two studies a month apart: one varies engine thrust, the other
      ship mass. Because both draw three vehicles’ paths, speed, acceleration
      and turning acceleration on identical axes, a reader can place a thrust
      result beside a mass result and see which matters more.
    </p>
    <p>It follows that the instrument must be:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Standard.</strong>
        Measurement choices belong to the instrument, not to each study.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A Lab tool.</strong>
        Full screen, with configurations chosen and variables tried in one
        collapsible side pane that keeps the data on stage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Embeddable.</strong>
        It treats its container as a screen at least as large as an iPad Pro. In
        the Lab it fills the container; in a study it takes the full container
        width and snaps to the center of the flowing text as the reader scrolls.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The tension is between a measurement fixed enough to compare and an
      instrument flexible enough to be useful across questions. This cycle
      settles what is fixed and what a study may vary. Simulating flight, engine
      tuning and production values stay outside it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="exploration">
    <h2 id="exploration">02 / What the instrument decides</h2>
    <h3>02.1 / The same two studies, measured two ways</h3>
    <p>
      Two small studies run the same calculation: one varies acceleration, the
      other the speed ceiling. Each is drawn first with axes fitted to its own
      data, as studies have done, then on one fixed instrument. The calculation
      is the existing free-rotation path for the “2 aft + 2 side” fixture on the
      shared 14-second input; the drawings are static.
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice whether a reader can tell, from the top row, which change moved the
      ship more. Then notice what the bottom row makes visible, and where a
      value runs off the fixed range. The fixed ranges here are provisional, and
      the example cannot say how a reader will feel about them.
    </p>
    <PathComparisonRows />
    <h3>Settled from 02.1</h3>
    <p>
      A path view needs two formats, switched by a toggle. One auto-zooms to
      show every path in the run. The other uses the instrument’s default scale,
      which is the same in every study, and lets the reader move around it with
      the mouse. This applies to the path view only. The fixed scales for the
      speed, acceleration and turn traces, and the numbers chosen here, remain
      provisional.
    </p>
    <h3>02.2 / Fit all, or default scale and pan</h3>
    <p>
      The same three paths from Study B, in a path view that switches between
      the two formats. In default scale the view is larger than the screen can
      hold, so the paths run off the edge and the reader pans to follow them.
      Dragging works with the mouse; arrow buttons and Recenter cover the
      keyboard.
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice how much of the run the default scale shows at once, and whether
      panning feels like reading or like searching. The example does not decide
      the default scale value, a zoom control, or how the trace views follow a
      pan; only the path view is under test.
    </p>
    <PathViewDemo client:load />
    <p>
      <strong>Observed:</strong>
      both formats work as they are. Showing every path, or showing them on a
      standard scale that is easier to understand, is enough for the path view,
      and it does not need more fidelity. The default scale value stays
      undecided.
    </p>
    <h3>02.3 / A time slider across every view</h3>
    <p>
      The drawings are still static, but a slider under the path area sets a
      moment in time. A marker appears on every vehicle’s path, a cursor line
      crosses the speed, acceleration and turn traces and the input strip, and
      the table gives the values at that moment. It works like the cursor of a
      measuring tool, not a playback control: nothing moves until the reader
      moves it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice whether you can find where a vehicle was when a trace changes, and
      whether a tenth of a second or a hundredth is the useful step. In default
      scale a marker can sit off screen until the view is panned to it. The
      example does not decide the time resolution, trace scaling, or how the
      controls sit in the side pane.
    </p>
    <PathTraceDemo client:load />
    <p>
      <strong>Observed:</strong>
      the slider works for following the vehicles across the views, and both a
      tenth and a hundredth of a second are useful steps. A thin tick at 90° to
      the path at each marker shows the path’s direction at that moment; moving
      the slider shows which way the vehicle travels along it. The slider
      follows the shared range-input pattern the studies already use; turning it
      into a design-system component is a candidate for after the study.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="next">
    <h2 id="next">03 / Not reached</h2>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
ul {
  max-width: 70ch;
  margin-block: 0 var(--spacing-md);
}
li + li {
  margin-top: var(--spacing-sm);
}
</style>
